    {
      "id": "q1",
      "label": "Question 1",
      "prompt": "Which of the following best describes compliance as a type of conformity?\n\nA. Publicly changing behaviour while privately disagreeing\nB. Publicly and privately changing behaviour permanently\nC. Privately accepting the majority view but not changing behaviour\nD. Changing behaviour only when the majority is absent",
      "marks": 1,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "label": "Question 2",
      "prompt": "Outline the difference between identification and internalisation as types of conformity.",
      "marks": 2,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "label": "Question 3",
      "prompt": "A student joins a new sixth form and starts wearing the same style of clothes as the rest of her friendship group because she wants to be liked by them.\n\nExplain which explanation of conformity best accounts for the student's behaviour.",
      "marks": 3,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "label": "Question 4",
      "prompt": "Briefly evaluate research into social influence carried out in laboratory settings.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "label": "Question 5",
      "prompt": "A researcher investigated conformity by asking participants to estimate how many dots were shown on a screen. Participants gave their estimates individually, then again after hearing a confederate give a much higher estimate. Overall, 34% of participants' second estimates moved towards the confederate's figure.\n\nExplain how informational social influence could account for the participants' changed estimates.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "label": "Question 6",
      "prompt": "Outline the dispositional explanation for obedience.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "label": "Question 7",
      "prompt": "A hospital receptionist is asked by a senior consultant to enter patient data into a system she has not been trained to use. She does so because the consultant is wearing a white coat, works in the office next to the medical director and speaks with authority about hospital policy.\n\nReferring to the receptionist's behaviour, explain two features of the agentic state.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "label": "Question 8",
      "prompt": "Discuss research into the effect of situational variables on obedience.",
      "marks": 8,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "label": "Question 9",
      "prompt": "A factory manager wants to reduce the number of workers who follow unsafe instructions from an overbearing shift supervisor. She plans to introduce a short training session in which workers hear from a confident, likeable former employee who now works as a safety inspector.\n\nUsing your knowledge of resistance to social influence, explain two features of this plan that are likely to make the message effective.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "label": "Question 10",
      "prompt": "Explain how social support can help people to resist the pressure to conform.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "label": "Question 11",
      "prompt": "* Discuss minority influence. Your answer should include research evidence and consideration of the processes involved.",
      "marks": 16,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "label": "Question 12",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is a feature of the multi-store model of memory?\n\nA. Rehearsal transfers information between the short-term and long-term stores\nB. The short-term store has unlimited capacity\nC. Encoding is mainly acoustic in the long-term store\nD. Sensory memory holds information for several minutes",
      "marks": 1,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "label": "Question 13",
      "prompt": "A student reads a phone number aloud while walking to class, but forgets it as soon as a friend asks her a question.\n\nExplain the student's forgetting using the concept of displacement.",
      "marks": 2,
      "assets": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "label": "Question 14",
      "prompt": "Outline what psychological research has shown about the capacity of short-term memory.",
      "marks": 4,
      "assets": []
